help mtefe                                                                     (SJ18-1: st00!!)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Title

    mtefe -- Marginal treatment effects with factor variables


Syntax

        mtefe depvar [indepvars] (depvar_t = varlist_iv) [if] [in] [weight] [, options]

    options                    Description
    -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    Model
      polynomial(#)            specify the degree of the polynomial for the polynomial
                                 models; default is the joint normal
      splines(numlist)         allow for splines at knots specified by numlist in parametric
                                 models
      semiparametric           specify the semiparametric model
      restricted(varlist)      control variables restricted to be the same in treated and
                                 untreated state
      link(string)             link function can be probit, logit, or lpm; default is
                                 link(probit)

    Estimation
      separate                 fit the model using the separate approach rather than local
                                 instrumental variables (LIVs)
      mlikelihood              fit the model using maximum likelihood rather than local IVs;
                                 only for joint normal model
      trimsupport(#)           trim off # share of the treated and untreated sample from the
                                 thinnest tails of support
      fullsupport              estimate marginal treatment effect (MTE) over the full unit
                                 interval even when using semiparametric models
      prte(varname)            estimate policy-relevant treatment effects based on the
                                 propensity scores in varname

    Inference
      bootreps(#)              use bootstrap with # repetitions
      norepeat1                do not reestimate the propensity scores, mean of X, and
                                 parameter weights for each replication
      vce(vcetype)             standard error specification -- robust or cluster clustvar
      level(#)                 specify confidence level

    Semiparametric
      degree(#)                specify degree of local polynomial smooth in semiparametric
                                 models
      ybwidth(#)               specify bandwidth of local polynomial smooth for the outcome
                                 in semiparametric models
      xbwidth(#)               specify bandwidth of local polynomial smooth for X variables
                                 in the semiparametric model
      gridpoints(#)            evaluate the local polynomial smooths of X on p at # points
                                 rather than precise propensity scores
      kernel(string)           kernel for use in the semiparametric models

    Other
      first                    report the first-step estimates
      second                   report the underlying second-stage (rarely used)
      noplot                   do not graph common support and MTE plots
      savefirst(string)        save the first-stage estimates to disk as string.ster
      savepropensity(newvar)   save the estimated propensity scores in a new variable newvar
      savekp                   save the variables of the K(p) [and K0(p)] functions in the
                                 dataset for postestimation
      saveweights(string)      save the weights for the mean of X in in the subpopulations
                                 for the treatment effect parameters in variables with prefix
                                 string
      norescale                Does not rescale the weights of the treatment effect
                                 parameters to sum to 1 in cases with limited support.
    -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    fweights and pweights are allowed unless bootstrapping; see weight.


Description

    mtefe calculates MTEs derived using a variety of parametric and semiparametric models and
    three estimation methods.


Options

        +-------+
    ----+ Model +----------------------------------------------------------------------------

    polynomial(#) specifies the k(p) or k_j(p) functions to be polynomials of degree #.
        Required for the parametric and semiparametric polynomial models.  Note that mtefe
        specifies the degree of the MTE directly, rather than the degree of K(p) like margte,
        so a polynomial of L+1 in margte will be equivalent to L in mtefe.

    splines(numlist) allows for splines with knots at k1, k2, ... kQ as specified in numlist.
        Specifies splines for degree 2 and above in k(p) or k_j(p).  Required for models with
        splines. Degree of polynomial must be greater than 1.

    semiparametric specifies that the models be fit using semiparametric methods --
        semiparametric polynomial, semiparametric polynomial with splines, or semiparametric
        using double residual regression.

    restricted(varlist) specifies control variables that are included in the first stage and
        second stage, but these are restricted to have the same effect in the treated and
        untreated state.

    link(string) specifies the first-stage model. string may be probit, logit, or lpm.  The
        default is link(probit).

        +------------+
    ----+ Estimation +-----------------------------------------------------------------------

    separate specifies that the model be fit using the separate approach or principled
        stratification. Otherwise, estimation is done using local IVs unless
        {cmd_mlikelihood} is specified.

    mlikelihood specifies that the model be fit using maximum likelihood.  This is
        appropriate only for the standard normal model.  The evaluator is an adapted version
        of the maximum likelihood evaluator from Lokshin and Sajaia (2004), see movestay.

    trimsupport(#) trims the sample after estimating the propensity scores to deal with
        issues of common support. After estimating the propensity scores, this option
        estimates the density of p separately in each sample. It then removes individuals
        from the points of least support until a total share # of each sample has been
        removed. Common support is calculated at the points of the propensity score
        distribution where the two samples have overlapping support, estimation proceeds
        excluding the individuals with estimated scores outside the range of the relevant
        subsample and the first stage is re-estimated on the trimmed sample. This procedure
        drops exactly # share of the total sample, but the final estmation sample will
        include observations outside the common support in the fat tail of each subsample of
        treated and control.

    fullsupport specifies that the MTE is estimated over the full unit interval even when
        using semiparametric models.  By default, the MTE will only be estimated at points of
        overlapping support in the treated and untreated samples. Therefore, using
        fullsupport relies on extrapolation in the local linear regressions, and may be
        unstable.  This option has no effect when using parametric models. Use with caution.

    prte(varname) fits the specified MTE model and calculates the PRTE for a hypothetical
        policy change that generated the propensity score distribution in variable varname.

        +-----------+
    ----+ Inference +------------------------------------------------------------------------

    bootreps(#) uses the bootstrap for inference, performing # bootstrap replications.
        Otherwise, analytic standard errors are calculated, ignoring the estimation error
        from the first stage. See the remark on standard errors below.

    norepeat1 specifies that the first stage, treatment effect parameter weights and means of
        X are not reestimated for each bootstrap repetition, thus ignoring the estimation
        error from the first stage in the bootstrap. See the remark on standard errors below.

    vce(vcetype) specifies standard errors -- robust or cluster clustvar. Works for both
        analytic standard errors and bootstrapped ones.

    level(#) uses a confidence level of #% for confidence intervals and MTE plots. The
        default is level(95).

        +----------------+
    ----+ Semiparametric +-------------------------------------------------------------------

    degree(#) specifies the degree of the local polynomial regressions used in the
        semiparametric models. When using the semiparametric polynomial model, this option is
        overruled by the degree specified in the polynomial() option plus one, so this option
        is relevant only for the semiparametric model.  The degree of polynomial smooth is
        always 1 for the local polynomial regressions of the covariates on p, so this affects
        only the degree in the local polynomial regression of Y on p. The default is
        degree(2).

    ybwidth(#) specifies the bandwidth of the local polynomial smooth of Y on p in
        semiparametric models. The default is ybwidth(0.2).

    xbwidth(#) specifies the bandwidth of the local polynomial smooth of the covariates on p
        in semiparametric models. The default is lpoly's rule-of-thumb.

    gridpoints(#) is used with the fully semiparametric model only. Instead of running the
        local polynomial regressions of covariates on p at each and every estimated
        propensity score, this option allows for estimating it at # separate points spread
        out between the minimum and maximum of the propensity score distribution and then
        assigns every individual the predicted value from the closest point in this set
        before calculating the residuals. In large samples, this may provide large
        improvement in computation time.  Does not affect the local polynomial regressions of
        Y on p that are always performed at the points of common support.

    kernel(string) uses string as the kernel in all local polynomial regressions. string may
        be epanechnikov, biweight, cosine, gaussian, parzen, rectangle, or triangle. The
        default is kernel(epanechnikov).

        +-------+
    ----+ Other +----------------------------------------------------------------------------

    first reports the first-stage estimates.

    second reports the output from the second-stage estimating equation, which might not be
        particularly easy to read because of temporary variables.  Use primarily for
        investigating the log-likelihood iterations when using maximum likelihood.

    noplot suppresses the drawing of common support and MTE plots.

    norescale specifies that the treatment effect parameter weights are not rescaled to sum
        to 1 in cases with limited support. When support is limited, regular non-marginal
        treatment effect parameters (ATE, ATT, ATUT, LATE and PRTE) cannot be estimated.
        Instead, mtefe by default rescales the weights to sum to 1 within the support,
        creating a parameter that can be interpreted analogously to the original, but for
        people with unobserved resistance within the support. If norescale is specified
        instead, the weights are not rescaled.

    savefirst(string) saves the first-stage estimates to disk using the filename string.ster.
        If suboption margins is specified, mtefe instead saves the marginal effects for all
        variables.

    savepropensity(newvar) saves the predicted propensity scores from the first stage to a
        new variable called newvar.

    savekp saves the variables used in K(p) and, if separate or mlikelihood is specified,
        K0(p) in the dataset for postestimation use.  Relevant only for parametric models.

    saveweights(string) saves the estimated weights for mean of X in the relevant
        subopulations for the treatment effect parameters in variables with the prefix
        string. Weights are calculated for the ATT, ATUT, LATE, MPRTE1 and, if specified in
        the option prte(), PRTE.

Remarks

    The model

    The MTEs are derived from the generalized Roy model

          Y = (1 - D)Y_0 + DY_1
        Y_j = Xb_j + U_j for j=0,1
          D = 1(Zg>V) for Z=X,Z_

    where Y_1 is the outcome in the treated state, Y_0 is the outcome in the untreated state,
    D is a binary treatment indicator, and 1[A] is the indicator function for event A. Zg is
    an index that can be interpreted as a latent utility of treatment.

    Transforming the treatment condition gives P(Z)>U_d, where P(Z) is the propensity score,
    or the probability of treatment given Z, and U_d=F(V) is the percentiles of V, the
    unobserved resistance to treatment.

    Estimation of MTE requires the (standard) IV assumptions of conditional independence of
    the errors (U_0,U_1,V) from Z_ conditional on X.  This allows us to estimate the MTE
    within the support of P(Z) conditional on X. In practical applications, however, this
    support is often very limited, and most applied work therefore require an additional
    separability assumption: E(U_j | X,V) = E(U_j | V).  This last assumption implies that
    the level of MTE, but not the slope, is a function of X and allows us to identify the MTE
    over the unconditional support of P(Z). In many cases, even the unconditional support of
    P(Z) can be limited, and the MTE can only be estimated for parts of the unit interval.
    See the note on support below for how mtefe treats these cases.

    The MTE measures the average treatment effect for people at a particular margin of
    indifference, or with a particular resistance to treatment. It can be expressed as

        MTE(x,u) = E(Y_1-Y_0|X=x,U_d=u) = X(b_1-b_0)+k(u)

    where

        k(u) = E(U_1-U_0|U_d=u)

    We can trace out the distribution of the MTE over U_d, MTE(x,u), within the support of p.


    Estimation using local IV

    It can be shown that the derivative of the conditional expectation of Y with respect to p
    identifies the MTE (Heckman and Vytlacil 2007a,b). Given the separability assumption, we
    have

        E{Y|X,P(Z)=p} = Xb_0 + X(b_1-b_0)p +K(p)

    where

        K(p) = pE(U_1 - U_0|U_d < p)

    Thus, if we make assumptions on the K(p) function, we can estimate the conditional
    expectation of Y and form its derivative to construct the MTE.


    Estimation using the separate approach

    When using the separate approach, we estimate the conditional expectation separately in
    the treated and untreated sample. We apply the appropriate selection correction,
    depending on the model, to account for the endogenous selection into treatment. Following
    the notation of Brinch, Mogstad, and Wiswall (2017),

        E(Y_j|X,D=j,p) = Xb_j + K_j(p)

    where

        K_1(p) = E(U_1|U_d < p)
        K_0(p) = E(U_0|U_d >= p)

    Thus, if we make assumptions on the K_j(p) functions, we can estimate the conditional
    expectation. Then, we form the MTE as

        MTE(x,u)=E(Y_1-Y_0|X = x, U_d = u) = X(b_1-b_0)+k_1(p)-k_0(p)

    where

        k_1(p) = K_1(p)+pK_1'(p)
        k_0(p) = K_0(p)-(1-p)K_0'(p)

    As with the local IV method, the exact specification of the K-functions depends on the
    model.


    Estimation using maximum likelihood

    Alternatively, the joint normal MTE model can be estimated using maximum likelihood
    because we know the full distribution of the errors.  In that case, the log-likelihood
    function can be expressed as

        l=D[ln{F(eta_1)}+ln((1/s1)f(U_1/s1))] +(1-D)[ln((1/s0)f(U_0/s0))+ln{F(-eta_0)}]

    where f and F are standard normal density and cumulative density functions and

        eta_j = {Zg+(Y_j-Xb_j)*rho_j/s_j}/sqrt(1-rho_j^2)

    which can be maximized to find the parameters of the model. For details, see the movestay
    command, which inspired the log-likelihood estimation in mtefe, and Lokshin and Sajaia
    (2004).


    A note on standard errors

    Local IVs and the separate approach as implemented by mtefe are two-step methods.  In the
    first step, the propensity scores are estimated and predicted and the treatment-effect
    parameter weights are estimated based on these estimates. In the subsequent step, the
    rest of the parameters of the model are estimated, and lastly, the MTE estimates
    themselves are constructed from the estimates of b_1-b_0 and k(p).

    In this setting, analytic standard errors are inappropriate because they ignore the
    uncertainty from three sources: a) the uncertainty from the propensity score estimates by
    simply treating p as a variable, b) the uncertainty from the estimation of the
    treatment-effect parameter weights, and c) the uncertainty from the estimates of X, the
    set of values for which we evaluate the MTE and the treatment effect parameters.

    If we are interested in the MTE at the (unknown) mean of X in the population, it is
    appropriate to include the uncertainty in the estimate of the mean of X in the estimates
    of the MTEs.

    For all these reasons, the most conservative way to estimate standard errors of MTE
    models are through the bootstrap. The bootstrap in mtefe takes all the above mentioned
    sources of uncertainty into account by reestimating the first step for each bootstrap
    replication, unless the options norepeat1 is specified.

    Nonetheless, the default standard errors reported by mtefe are calculated analytically,
    ignoring these sources of uncertainty. Users are warned about this after estimation
    unless bootstrap is applied with the bootreps() option. When cluster bootstrapping the
    standard errors (using bootreps() and the vce(cluster clustvar) options together), users
    should be wary of specifications that also include the cluster variable in one of the
    independent variable lists. mtefe handles the most common application of this, when the
    cluster variable is included as fixed effects in one of the independent variable lists,
    but with other (arguable uncommon) specifications such as the cluster variable included
    as a continuous regressor and a fixed effect, users should be careful.

    When using maximum likelihood estimation of the joint normal model, all model parameters
    are estimated in one step. This implies that the analytic standard errors are more
    appropriate, because they now account for the uncertainty of the first-step estimates,
    but they still do not account for the uncertainty in the estimation of the mean or the
    estimates of the treatment-effect parameter weights.


    A note on the support of P(Z)

    Under the separability assumption, the MTE can be identified semiparametrically over the
    support of P(Z).  If the support is limited, identification outside the common support
    will necessarily rely on extrapolation.  By default, mtefe estimates the MTE over the
    full unit interval if a parametric model is specified.  If a semiparametric model is
    specified, however, mtefe estimates the MTE only at points where there are at least one
    treated and one untreated individual, unless the option fullsupport is specified.  In
    addition, the option trimsupport() allows the user to trim a fraction of # from the tails
    of each sample, in practice strengthening the conditions for what constitutes support.
    Individuals with propensity scores outside the support for the relevant sample are
    dropped, and the first stage is reestimated when trimsupport() is specified. This option
    trumps the fullsupport option.

    In cases where the MTE is not estimated over the full unit interval, conventional
    non-marginal treatment effect parameters (ATE, ATT, ATUT, LATE and PRTE) cannot be
    estimated, as they depend on the MTE over the full unit interval. In these cases, the
    user should be careful when interpreting the treatment effect estimates.  mtefe by
    default rescales the treatment effect parameter weights to sum to 1 within the support,
    producing a parameter that can be interpreted analogously to the original but for people
    with unobserved resistance within the support. These parameters are identical to the
    originals only if the weighted average over the MTE outside of the support is equal to
    the weighted average within the support. Alternatively, if norescale is specified, mtefe
    does not rescale the weights when producing the parameter estimates. This parameter is
    equal to the original only if the MTE is equal to 0 outside of the support.

Examples

    To fix ideas, the mtefe_gendata program simulates data on log wages for people with
    varying experience, living in different districts or labor markets, and with and without
    college education. College education is endogenous, so we need an instrument to identify
    causal effects.  Unfortunately, even the distance to college is not a valid instrument
    because there is correlation across districts; rural labor markets with longer distances
    to colleges are different than urban ones. Within districts, however, the distance to
    college is unrelated to unobservables, so the instrument is valid conditional on fixed
    effects.

    Generate simulated data of 10,000 observations distributed in 10 districts
        . mtefe_gendata, obs(10000) districts(10)

    The parametric normal model, fit using local IV
        . mtefe lwage exp exp2 i.district (col=distCol)

    The parametric polynomial of degree 1, estimated using the separate approach
        . mtefe lwage exp exp2 i.district (col=distCol), separate pol(1)

    The polynomial model of degree 2, using a linear probability model for the propensity
    score
        . mtefe lwage exp exp2 i.district (col=distCol), polynomial(2) link(lpm)

    The parametric normal model, fit using maximum likelihood
        . mtefe lwage exp exp2 i.district (col=distCol), mlikelihood

    The semiparametric polynomial model with 50 bootstrap replications
        . mtefe lwage exp exp2 i.district (col=distCol), pol(1) bootreps(50)

    The joint normal model where the fixed effects are restricted to be the same in the
    treated and untreated state (note: violation of exclusion)
        . mtefe lwage exp exp2 (col=distCol), restricted(i.district)

    The semiparametric model, using a smaller evaluation grid to save computational time
        . mtefe lwage exp exp2 i.district (col=distCol), semiparametric gridpoints(100)

    Calculating the PRTE for a policy that mandates a maximum distance to college of 40
    miles, normal model
        . probit col distCol exp exp2 i.district
        . rename distCol tempdistcol
        . gen distCol=min(40,tempdistcol)
        . predict double p_prte
        . drop distCol
        . rename tempdistcol distCol
        . mtefe lwage exp exp2 i.district (col=distCol), prte(p_prte)
        . mtefeplot, prte

    The polynomial model of degree 2 with two splines at 0.25 and 0.75, fit using the local
    IV
        . mtefe lwage exp exp2 i.district (col=distCol), polynomial(2) splines(0.25 0.75)


Stored results

    mtefe stores the following in e():

    Scalars        
      e(N)                number of observations
      e(IV)               regular two-stage least-squares estimate
      e(polynomial)       degree of polynomial in polynomial models
      e(p_U)              p-value for a test of unobserved heterogeneity -- the coefficients
                            on k(p)
      e(p_X)              p-value for a test of observed heterogeneity -- the b_1-b_0
                            coefficients

    Macros         
      e(cmd)              mtefe
      e(cmdline)          command as typed
      e(depvar)           name of dependent variable
      e(title)            title in estimation output
      e(title2)           secondary title in estimation output
      e(method)           estimation method
      e(properties)       b V; V

    Matrices       
      e(b)                coefficient vector, including MTE and treatment parameters
      e(V)                variance-covariance matrix of b, empty if using semiparametric
                            methods and no bootstrap
      e(trimminglimits)   trimming limits if using the trimsupport() option.
      e(support)          vector of common support over p
      e(mte)              vector of MTE estimates
      e(mtexs_ate)        vector of x values for which the main MTE and ATE were calculated
      e(mteatt)           MTE curve for the treated individuals
      e(weightsatt)       estimated weights at each point of support for the ATT
      e(mtexs_att)        vector of x values for which the ATT was calculated
      e(mteatut)          MTE curve for the untreated individuals
      e(weightsatut)      estimated weights at each point of support for the ATUT
      e(mtexs_atut)       vector of x values for which the ATUT was calculated
      e(mtelate)          MTE curve for the compliers
      e(weightslate)      estimated weights at each point of support for the LATE
      e(mtexs_atut)       vector of x values for which the LATE was calculated
      e(mteprte)          MTE curve for the policy compliers if specified in prte()
      e(weightsprte)      estimated weights at each point of support for the PRTE (if
                            specified in prte())
      e(mtexs_atut)       vector of x values for which the PRTE was calculated (if specified
                            in prte())
      e(dkdp)             estimates of k(u) at the points of support
      e(weightsmprte1)    estimated weights at each point of support for the MPRTE1 parameter
      e(weightsmprte2)    estimated weights at each point of support for the MPRTE2 parameter
      e(weightsmprte3)    estimated weights at each point of support for the MPRTE3 parameter
      e(tescales)         scales of the non-marginal treatment effect parameters in
                            situations with limited support

    Functions      
      e(sample)           marks estimation sample

    If semiparametric is specified, the following matrices are stored:

    Matrices       
      e(bandwidth)        bandwidths used in local polynomial smooths, alternatively as
                            e(bandwidth1) and e(bandwidth0) if the separate approach is used
      e(degree)           degree in local polynomial smooth

    If separate or mlikelihood is specified, the following matrices are stored:

    Matrices       
      e(Y1)               average outcomes in the treated state
      e(Y0)               average outcomes in the untreated state

    Note: bootstrap stores its own output in e() as well.  See bootstrap.

    Note: Various options store results as variables as well. These include saveweights(),
    where the variable prefix is user specified; savepropensity, where the new variable name
    is user specified; and savekp, where the variable names are predetermined to work with
    postestimation predictions.




